\vspace{0.5cm}

A \textbf{Hamiltonian path} visits every \textbf{vertex} exactly once.

A \textbf{Hamiltonian circuit} is a Hamiltonian path that returns to
the starting vertex (a cycle through all vertices).

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key difference from Euler:}}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
 & \textbf{Euler} & \textbf{Hamiltonian} \\
\hline
Must visit & Every \textbf{edge} once & Every \textbf{vertex} once \\
\hline
May skip & Vertices & Edges \\
\hline
Easy test? & \textbf{Yes} (degree condition) &
             \textbf{No} (NP-complete) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Sufficient conditions
(not necessary):}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item \textbf{Dirac's Theorem:} If $n \geq 3$ and every vertex
          has degree $\geq n/2$, a Hamiltonian circuit exists.
    \item \textbf{Ore's Theorem:} If every pair of non-adjacent
          vertices $u,v$ satisfies $\deg(u)+\deg(v) \geq n$,
          a Hamiltonian circuit exists.
\end{itemize}

