\textbf{(a) [2022 Q1(ix)]} State the condition for an Euler circuit.
Does $G$ have one? Does it have an Euler path?

\textbf{(b) [2023 Q1(vi)]} Define Hamiltonian circuit. Does $G$ have
one? List it.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{(a)} \textbf{Condition for Euler circuit:} Connected and
every vertex has even degree.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|l|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Vertex} & \textbf{Incident edges} & $\deg$ & \textbf{Parity} \\
\hline
1 & $(1,2),(1,3)$ & 2 & Even \\
2 & $(1,2),(2,3),(2,4)$ & 3 & \textcolor{red}{Odd} \\
3 & $(1,3),(2,3),(3,4),(3,5)$ & 4 & Even \\
4 & $(2,4),(3,4),(4,5)$ & 3 & \textcolor{red}{Odd} \\
5 & $(3,5),(4,5)$ & 2 & Even \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Vertices 2 and 4 have odd degree. \textbf{No Euler circuit.}

Exactly 2 odd-degree vertices $\Rightarrow$ an \textbf{Euler path
exists}, starting at vertex 2 and ending at vertex 4 (or vice versa).

One Euler path: $2 \to 1 \to 3 \to 2 \to 4 \to 3 \to 5 \to 4$

\textbf{(b)} A \textbf{Hamiltonian circuit} is a closed path that
visits every vertex of the graph \textbf{exactly once} before
returning to the start.

Try: $1 \to 2 \to 4 \to 5 \to 3 \to 1$

Check all edges exist: $(1,2)\checkmark$, $(2,4)\checkmark$,
$(4,5)\checkmark$, $(5,3)\checkmark$, $(3,1)\checkmark$

\textbf{Hamiltonian circuit exists}: $1{-}2{-}4{-}5{-}3{-}1$

\vspace{0.7cm}

\textbf{Euler's Formula} (for any connected planar graph):
\[
    v - e + f = 2
\]
where $v=$ vertices, $e=$ edges, $f=$ faces (regions bounded by
edges, including the \textbf{one unbounded outer region}).

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Planarity inequality} (necessary condition for $v \geq 3$):
\[
    e \leq 3v - 6
\]
If this is violated, the graph is \textbf{definitely not planar}.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Non-planar graphs:}}
$K_5$ and $K_{3,3}$ are the two simplest non-planar graphs
(Kuratowski's theorem).

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textbf{Chromatic number} $\chi(G)$: the \textbf{minimum} number
of colours needed to colour every vertex so that no two
\textbf{adjacent} vertices share a colour.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Graph} & \textbf{Why} & $\chi(G)$ \\
\hline
Any tree & Bipartite (2-colourable) & 2 \\
\hline
$K_{m,n}$ (bipartite) & Two partitions; colour each one colour & 2 \\
\hline
$C_n$ even cycle & 2-colourable & 2 \\
\hline
$C_n$ odd cycle & 3rd colour needed at last vertex & 3 \\
\hline
$K_n$ complete graph & Every pair adjacent; all different & $n$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

