\vspace{0.7cm}

Let $G$ be an undirected graph with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges.
Label vertices $v_1, \ldots, v_n$ and edges $e_1, \ldots, e_m$.

The \textbf{incidence matrix} $M$ is an $n \times m$ matrix where:
\[
    M_{ij} =
    \begin{cases}
        1 & \text{if vertex } v_i \text{ is an endpoint of edge }
              e_j \\
        0 & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
\]

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key properties:}}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|p{8.5cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Plain explanation} \\
\hline
\textbf{Size} & $n \times m$ --- NOT square (unlike adjacency matrix) \\
\hline
\textbf{Column sum} & Every column sums to exactly \textbf{2}
                      (every edge has exactly 2 endpoints) \\
\hline
\textbf{Row sum} & Row $i$ sum $= \deg(v_i)$ \\
\hline
\textbf{Loop} & A loop at $v_i$ gives a column with only
                one entry: $M_{ij}=1$ at row $i$
                (or sometimes 2, check your convention) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Key difference from adjacency matrix:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Adjacency matrix: $n \times n$, tells you which
          \textbf{vertices} are connected.
    \item Incidence matrix: $n \times m$, tells you which
          \textbf{edges} touch which vertices.
\end{itemize}

