\vspace{0.5cm}

Two simple graphs $G_1 = (V_1, E_1)$ and $G_2 = (V_2, E_2)$ are
\textbf{isomorphic}, written $G_1 \cong G_2$, if there exists a
\textbf{bijection} (one-to-one and onto)
$f: V_1 \to V_2$ such that:
\[
    (u,\,v) \in E_1
    \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad
    \bigl(f(u),\,f(v)\bigr) \in E_2
\]

In plain words: $u$ and $v$ are adjacent in $G_1$
\textbf{if and only if} their images $f(u)$ and $f(v)$ are
adjacent in $G_2$.

The function $f$ is called an \textbf{isomorphism}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ What isomorphism
means in practice:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item The graphs have the \textbf{same number of vertices}.
    \item The graphs have the \textbf{same number of edges}.
    \item Every connection is \textbf{preserved} under $f$ ---
          edges map to edges and non-edges map to non-edges.
    \item The graphs have the same
          \textbf{degree sequence} (sorted list of all degrees).
\end{itemize}

