\vspace{0.6cm}

A \textbf{graph invariant} is a property preserved under
isomorphism. If $G_1 \cong G_2$ then they must share every
invariant. Contrapositive: if any invariant differs, the graphs
are \textbf{immediately not isomorphic}.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Standard invariant checklist
(use in order):}}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|l|p{6.2cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Step} & \textbf{Invariant} & \textbf{How to check} \\
\hline
1 & Number of vertices & $|V_1| = |V_2|$? \\
\hline
2 & Number of edges & $|E_1| = |E_2|$? \\
\hline
3 & Degree sequence & Sort all degrees; lists identical? \\
\hline
4 & Connected components & Same number? \\
\hline
5 & Number of triangles & Count cycles of length 3 \\
\hline
6 & Explicit bijection & Verify every edge maps to an edge \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Critical point:}}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Passing steps 1--5 does \textbf{not} prove isomorphism
          on its own --- you must find an explicit bijection.
    \item Failing \textbf{any one} step \textbf{immediately
          proves} the graphs are NOT isomorphic.
    \item The \textbf{degree sequence} (step 3) is the fastest
          and most powerful test --- always check it first.
\end{itemize}

