\textbf{Step 3 --- Structural analysis:}

In both graphs the two degree-3 vertices are \textbf{not directly
adjacent} to each other (check: $(u_2,u_4)\notin E(A)$;
$(v_4,v_5)\notin E(B)$).

Each degree-3 vertex is adjacent to three degree-2 vertices.
The two degree-2 vertices adjacent to \textit{both} degree-3
vertices form a 4-cycle together with the degree-3 pair.
This matches in both graphs.

\textbf{Step 4 --- Construct the bijection $f$:}

Match degree-3 vertices: $f(u_2) = v_4$, \; $f(u_4) = v_5$.

Neighbours of $u_2$: $\{u_1, u_3, u_6\}$.
Neighbours of $v_4$: $\{v_1, v_2, v_3\}$.

Neighbours of $u_4$: $\{u_1, u_3, u_5\}$.
Neighbours of $v_5$: $\{v_1, v_3, v_6\}$.

Shared neighbours of $u_2$ and $u_4$: $\{u_1, u_3\}$.
Shared neighbours of $v_4$ and $v_5$: $\{v_1, v_3\}$.

Set $f(u_1)=v_1$, $f(u_3)=v_3$, $f(u_5)=v_6$, $f(u_6)=v_2$.

\textbf{Bijection:}
\[
f:\quad u_1\mapsto v_1,\;\; u_2\mapsto v_4,\;\;
        u_3\mapsto v_3,\;\; u_4\mapsto v_5,\;\;
        u_5\mapsto v_6,\;\; u_6\mapsto v_2
\]

\textbf{Step 5 --- Verify all 7 edges of A map to edges of B:}

