A connected graph $G$ has an \textbf{Euler circuit} if and only if
every vertex has \textbf{even degree}:
\[
    G \text{ has an Euler circuit}
    \;\iff\;
    \deg(v) \text{ is even for every } v \in V
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}

A connected graph $G$ has an \textbf{Euler path} (but not an
Euler circuit) if and only if it has
\textbf{exactly two vertices of odd degree}.
\[
    G \text{ has an Euler path (not circuit)}
    \;\iff\;
    \text{exactly 2 vertices have odd degree}
\]
The Euler path must \textbf{start at one odd-degree vertex}
and \textbf{end at the other}.

