\vspace{0.7cm}

Find $\chi(G)$ for each graph and give a proper coloring.

\textbf{(a)} $G_1 = C_5$ (the 5-cycle $1\!-\!2\!-\!3\!-\!4\!-\!5\!-\!1$)

\textbf{(b)} $G_2$: $V=\{a,b,c,d,e\}$,
$E=\{\{a,b\},\{a,c\},\{b,c\},\{c,d\},\{d,e\}\}$

\textbf{(c)} $G_3$: wheel graph $W_4$ (4-cycle with a central hub)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solutions:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(a) $C_5$:} $C_5$ is an odd cycle ($k=2$, so $2k+1=5$).
By Definition 5.14: $\chi(C_5) = 3$.

\textit{Why not 2?} A 2-coloring of any cycle must alternate
colors $A,B,A,B,\ldots$ When the cycle has odd length the last
vertex is adjacent to the first and both would receive color $A$.
Contradiction.

3-coloring: assign R, B, R, B, G to vertices $1,2,3,4,5$.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Edge} & \textbf{Colors} & \textbf{Different?} & \\
\hline
$\{1,2\}$ & R, B & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \\
$\{2,3\}$ & B, R & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \\
$\{3,4\}$ & R, B & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \\
$\{4,5\}$ & B, G & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \\
$\{5,1\}$ & G, R & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{$\chi(C_5) = 3$.}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b) $G_2$:} Vertices $\{a,b,c\}$ form a triangle
$\Rightarrow$ need at least 3 colors.

Color: $a=$ R, $b=$ B, $c=$ G (triangle).
$d$ is adjacent to $c$ (G) only $\Rightarrow$ $d=$ R.
$e$ is adjacent to $d$ (R) only $\Rightarrow$ $e=$ B.

3 colors suffice. Triangle forces $\chi \geq 3$.
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{$\chi(G_2) = 3$.}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c) $W_4$:} The outer 4-cycle is $C_4$ (even) needing
2 colors. The central hub is adjacent to all 4 outer vertices.
The outer cycle uses 2 colors; hub must differ from all of them
$\Rightarrow$ hub needs a 3rd color.

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{$\chi(W_4) = 3$.}}

(In general $\chi(W_n) = 3$ if $n$ is even, $\chi(W_n) = 4$
if $n$ is odd.)

\vspace{0.7cm}

A graph $G$ is \textbf{planar} if it can be drawn in the plane
so that \textbf{no two edges cross} (except at shared endpoints).
Such a drawing is called a \textbf{planar embedding}.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Planarity is a property of the \textit{graph itself} ---
          not of any particular drawing.
          A graph is planar if \textit{at least one} crossing-free
          drawing exists.
    \item A \textbf{face} is a connected region of the plane created
          by a planar embedding. This includes the one
          \textbf{outer (unbounded) face}.
    \item $f$ denotes the number of faces (always count the outer face).
\end{itemize}

