\vspace{0.2cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{(b) Properties for a graph to be a tree:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em, itemsep=1pt]
    \item Connected and no cycles.
    \item Connected with exactly $n-1$ edges.
    \item Exactly one simple path between any two vertices.
    \item Connected, but removing any edge makes it disconnected.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{(c) Binary Tree vs BST:}}

A \textbf{binary tree} has no rule on key ordering --- any value
can go anywhere as long as each node has at most 2 children.

A \textbf{BST} adds the ordering rule: left subtree $<$ node
$<$ right subtree at every node, which allows fast search.

