\vspace{0.7cm}

A \textbf{spanning tree} of a connected graph $G$ is a subgraph
that:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em, itemsep=1pt]
    \item Includes \textbf{all vertices} of $G$.
    \item Is \textbf{connected} and \textbf{acyclic}.
    \item Has exactly $\mathbf{n-1}$ edges.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.2cm}
A graph can have \textbf{many different} spanning trees.
Two standard methods to find one:

\vspace{0.15cm}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em, itemsep=1pt]
    \item \textbf{BFS} (Breadth-First Search) --- explore
          level by level.
    \item \textbf{DFS} (Depth-First Search) --- go as deep
          as possible before backtracking.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Past Paper:}} 2023 Q6

\vspace{0.4cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{BFS (Breadth-First Search):}}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em, itemsep=1pt]
    \item Start at a source vertex $s$. Mark it visited.
          Add to a \textbf{queue}.
    \item Dequeue vertex $u$. Visit all unvisited neighbours
          of $u$, mark them visited, add to queue.
    \item Repeat until queue is empty.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.2cm}
BFS visits vertices \textbf{level by level}.
The edges used to reach new vertices form the BFS spanning tree.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{DFS (Depth-First Search):}}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em, itemsep=1pt]
    \item Start at source $s$. Mark it visited.
          Push onto a \textbf{stack} (or use recursion).
    \item Visit an unvisited neighbour $v$ of the current
          vertex. Mark $v$ visited and move to $v$.
    \item If no unvisited neighbours exist, \textbf{backtrack}.
    \item Repeat until all vertices are visited.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.2cm}
DFS goes \textbf{deep first}, backtracking when stuck.
The edges used form the DFS spanning tree.

